u.s surgeon general jerome adams has  been meeting with community groups to  encourage measures like mask wearing and  social distancing  and overcome mistrust of vaccines first  on cbs this morning dr adams will also  tell us about a new effort  this is so important to protect pregnant  women and new moms so good morning to  you  mr surgeon general before we get to this  new effort i do want to talk about  the comments yesterday by cdc director  robert redfield he said we could be  facing  the most difficult period in the public  health history of this country  that actually echoes something he said  way back in april  that the president criticized him for  said he had been misquoted  when you think about those two data  points is there anything  that you wish the task force or you  yourself personally had said or done  differently to avoid this crisis that  we're in right now  well you ask a fair question and i think  i i wish that that i and i can only  speak for myself  had done a better job of communicating  the  uncertainty of this virus remember we're  still learning about it we're learning  new things every day and you saw new cdc  guidance on quarantine  and on testing come out and i have to  admit  that has confused the public when they  hear one thing one day  and it changes a few weeks or months  later but i want people to know that the  scientists are working hard around the  country at the cdc and at the fda  to give you the best recommendations  based on what we know now  and to continue to shift those  recommendations as the science warrants  and i want you to know  that we have great science that says  wearing a mask  and watching your distance and avoiding  contact outside of your household  will work to blunt this surge and  there's hope we're almost to a vaccine  so just hang on a little bit longer dr  adams i always appreciate your straight  talk on this so i want to pick up on one  more thing that dr redfield said he said  it was a problem his word a problem  that in the summer in july we were still  debating whether we should all be  wearing masks or not  out in public in addition to that  problem what else stood out to you about  the federal response to now  well one of the things that's  challenging in our country  is that we have a federalist system of  government  where all the states make up their own  minds and so one of the things we've  actively tried to do as doctors on the  task force  is go out to states and i'm high risk  myself but i've been to  uh i've been to states all over the  country been to cleveland uh been out to  uh  been out west to oklahoma been all over  the place trying to help people  understand their numbers on the ground  and we're seeing it work uh you hear a  lot of doom and gloom  but more people than ever are wearing  masks you saw holiday travel actually  decreased this year  compared to last year and so i want to  congratulate those people and tell  everyone else  hang on because we are in the midst of a  really bad surge  but uh what we do today will control  what happens weeks from now hang on  there is light at the end of the  tunnel dr adams it's good to see you i  want to talk about your call to action  to reduce maternal mortality in this  country i think many people would be  even surprised to hear that it is an  issue  what are you here to tell us about that  well gail thank you so much for the  opportunity we  have a coveted crisis in this country  but i want people to know we have a  maternal health  crisis in this country also with a woman  dying  a mom a mother-to-be dying every 12  hours  due to pregnancy-related complications  this is worse than many other developed  nations and it's getting worse because  of covet 19  but i want people to know this is  unacceptable but it is not inevitable  the cdc says  two-thirds of these deaths are  preventable and it's higher i'm told in  black women why  is that and what should we be doing  about it  well this is just shocking to me as a  father of a young woman as a husband of  a wife who's high risk  my sister right now is pregnant and she  is at high risk and i want people to  know  that black women in american indian  alaska native women are two to three  times more likely to die  versus white women and it doesn't matter  if you  have a phd like one of my own public  health service officers shalon irving  but why is that due to hypertension why  is that happening  well we know what's happening in many  cases because these women aren't being  heard and so i want to call out the  joint commission's  speak up campaign we're really trying  with this call to action to empower  women to speak up  but we're also telling people to listen  to women we know there's bias we know  there's  quite frankly racism and legacies of  racism  that mean people aren't heard and it  doesn't matter if you're beyonce  beyonce had a severe pre-eclampsia or  serena williams the best athlete in the  world  almost died due to pulmonary embolism we  need to do a deeper dive  into these root causes we need to really  empower women to speak up and we need to  listen to them so i want people to go to  surgeongeneral.gov  check out my call to action because it's  not just about the women it's about  what we do around them employers can  provide breastfeeding  and more supports for women we have the  best healthcare role to play  thank you dr adams he's right thank you  we haven't heard about serena williams  he is  yes we have the best best health care  system in the country but everyone needs  access to it equally  surgeon general thank you very much  jerome adams we appreciate it
 

